.TH INOTIFYD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
inotifyd \- run a program when the contents of a directory change
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B inotifyd
.I prog
.IR dir [\fB:\fImask\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B inotifyd
watches directories for changes.
When an entry in a watched directory changes in a way that the
directory's mask selects, it runs
.I prog
to report the change, and waits for
.I prog
to exit.
It runs until every watched directory has been deleted.
.PP
The name comes from the Linux inotify interface, but this
.B inotifyd
is a Windows implementation and differs from the Linux one; see
.BR NOTES .
It watches only directories, and only their direct entries, not the
contents of their subdirectories.
.SS Operands
.I prog
is the command to run for each change.
It is a single command name or path, not split into words or run by a
shell, so it cannot carry arguments of its own.
A
.I prog
of
.B \-
runs nothing and prints each change instead; see
.BR Output .
.PP
Each
.I dir
is an existing directory to watch, optionally followed by a colon and a
.IR mask .
A mask is a string of the event letters listed under
.BR Events ,
in any order and with repeats allowed.
It selects the events reported for that directory, and with no mask all
of them are.
Other characters in a mask are ignored.
A mask with no event letter, or with only
.BR x ,
is an error.
.PP
The mask follows the last colon in the operand, except that a colon in
the second position is a drive letter's.
Both
.B C:/work
and
.B C:/work:cn
are accepted, and the second has the mask
.BR cn .
A directory named by a single character cannot take a mask, because
.B d:c
is a path on drive D.
Write
.B ./d:c
instead.
.SS Running prog
.I prog
is run as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fIprog event dir\fR [\fIname\fR]
.EE
.RE
.PP
once for each event, with these arguments:
.TP
.I event
A letter that says what happened, from the list under
.BR Events .
.TP
.I dir
The operand as given, without the mask.
It is not made absolute or otherwise changed, so a relative
.I dir
is relative to the working directory of
.BR inotifyd ,
which
.I prog
also inherits.
.TP
.I name
The name of the entry that changed, without a directory part.
It is omitted when there is none.
.PP
.I prog
is found as for any command that busybox runs: an applet, a program in
.BR PATH ,
a file with a
.B #!\&
line, or a
.B .sh
file; see
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1).
Its standard input, standard output, standard error, and working
directory are those of
.BR inotifyd .
Its exit status is ignored.
If it cannot be run, nothing is printed and
.B inotifyd
carries on.
.PP
.B inotifyd
waits for each
.I prog
to exit before it looks at the next event.
Changes made in the meantime are queued, within the limit described in
.BR NOTES .
.SS Events
Each event is one of these letters.
One action can cause several events: creating and writing a file causes
an
.B n
event and then a
.B c
event, and a rename causes an
.B m
event and then a
.B y
event.
.TP
.B c
An entry was modified: its attributes, size, last write time, last
access time, creation time, or security descriptor changed.
.TP
.B d
An entry was deleted, or was moved out of the directory to another one.
.TP
.B m
An entry was renamed within the directory.
.I name
is the old name.
.TP
.B n
An entry was created, or was moved into the directory from another one.
.TP
.B x
The directory can no longer be watched, usually because it was deleted.
This ends the watch.
There is no
.IR name .
An
.B x
event is also reported when changes were lost, and that does not end the
watch; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B y
An entry was renamed within the directory.
.I name
is the new name.
.SS Output
With
.B \-
as
.IR prog ,
.B inotifyd
writes each event to standard output as a line of fields separated by
tabs: the
.IR event ,
the
.IR dir ,
and the
.IR name ,
which is left out, with its tab, when there is none.
Standard output is flushed after each line, so a pipe sees the events
as they happen.
.SH OPTIONS
.B inotifyd
takes no options.
Every argument is an operand, including one that begins with a dash, and
.B \-\-
has no special meaning.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when busybox starts, as needed
when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is, and a
.I dir
that is such a pattern is then not a directory.
When the variable is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, such as
.IR prog ,
so that they do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that busybox should not run when
.I prog
names them, so that a program in
.B PATH
runs instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B PATH
Where a
.I prog
with no directory part is looked up; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every watched directory was deleted, or the first argument was
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
Fewer than two operands, with a usage message on standard error; a mask
that selects no event; a
.I dir
that is not an existing directory; a directory that cannot be opened for
watching; or a failure while waiting for changes.
.PP
These errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
inotifyd: \fIdir\fR: invalid mask
inotifyd: \fIdir\fR: not a directory
inotifyd: add watch (\fIdir\fR) failed: \fIreason\fR
inotifyd: watch failed: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
The first of these is followed by an empty line.
The last is reported when waiting for changes fails, as it does at once
when there are more than 64 directories.
.PP
Otherwise
.B inotifyd
runs until it is interrupted, such as by Ctrl+C.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the changes to a directory as they happen.
Another console created
.I work/a.txt
and wrote to it, renamed it to
.IR b.txt ,
deleted it, and then deleted
.IR work ,
which ended
.BR inotifyd :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ inotifyd \- work
n	work	a.txt
c	work	a.txt
m	work	a.txt
y	work	b.txt
d	work	b.txt
x	work
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run
.B make
when a C source or header file in
.I src
is created, renamed to, or modified.
The script ignores other names:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat rebuild
#!/bin/sh
case $3 in
*.c|*.h) make ;;
esac
$ inotifyd ./rebuild src:cny
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print only new entries in one directory, and only deletions in another,
using a drive letter in the first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ inotifyd \- C:/spool:n archive:d
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
This
.B inotifyd
is built on the Windows function
.BR ReadDirectoryChangesW .
Differences from the Linux
.BR inotifyd :
.IP \(bu 2
Only directories can be watched, not files, and a watch does not extend
into subdirectories.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the events
.BR c ,
.BR d ,
.BR m ,
.BR n ,
.BR x ,
and
.B y
exist.
The Linux events
.BR a ,
.BR e ,
.BR w ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR r ,
.BR D ,
.BR M ,
.BR u ,
and
.B o
are ignored in a mask, and nothing is reported when a watched directory
is itself renamed.
.IP \(bu 2
.I prog
gets one event letter per run, never several together.
.PP
A rename within a directory is an
.B m
event and a
.B y
event.
A move between two directories is a
.B d
event in the one it left and an
.B n
event in the one it entered, even when both are watched.
.PP
Windows holds the changes that occur while
.I prog
runs in a buffer of 2048 bytes for each directory.
That is room for about 100 changes to entries with 4-character names,
and fewer with longer names.
When more changes arrive, all of those queued are discarded, and a
single
.B x
event with no name is reported in place of them, unless the mask leaves
out
.BR x .
Watching continues.
.PP
A deleted directory ends its watch, whether or not its mask reports
.BR x ,
and a new directory of the same name is not watched.
.B inotifyd
keeps a deleted directory open until it exits, so on older versions of
Windows its name stays in use until then.
A directory can be deleted or renamed while it is watched, and a
renamed one is still watched and still reported under the
name given on the command line.
At most 64 directories can be watched at once.
.PP
Entry names are converted to bytes with the ANSI code page, which is
UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later because busybox declares it
in its manifest.
A name that takes more than 260 bytes in that encoding is reported as if
it had no name, and one of exactly 260 bytes is followed by stray bytes.
.PP
A
.I dir
may use a drive letter and either kind of slash.
One that is a symbolic link or a junction is rejected as not a directory
unless it ends in a slash.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1)
